When filing for the registration of a new biological product, the requesting company must submit a single process containing the following documents in addition to the registration forms and payment proof:

*   Declaration of the company's size (economic capacity) [RDC 55/2010, Art. 30];
*   Copy of the Company's Operating License and/or Sanitary Permit [RDC 55/2010, Art. 30];
*   Copy of the Company's Authorization Certificate or its publication in the Official Gazette (DOU) [RDC 55/2010, Art. 30];
*   Copy of the updated Technical Responsibility Certificate issued by the Regional Pharmacy Council [RDC 55/2010, Art. 30];
*   Technical justification for the product registration [RDC 55/2010, Art. 30];
*   Copy of the Good Manufacturing Practices Certificate (CBPF) issued by Anvisa for all manufacturers of the active ingredient, bulk biological product, primary packaging, finished biological product, diluent, and adjuvant [RDC 55/2010, Art. 30].

Additionally, the company must provide clinical study protocols and reports, including:
*   Pharmacokinetic studies [RDC 55/2010, Art. 46];
*   Pharmacodynamic studies [RDC 55/2010, Art. 46];
*   Pivotal clinical safety and efficacy study(ies) [RDC 55/2010, Art. 46].

If the product is a biotechnological product, the company must also provide specific information regarding master and working cell banks (such as gene sequences, cloning methods, and stability data) and the characterization of the active substance [RDC 55/2010, Art. 34]. If there are multiple manufacturing sites, a comparative report of the manufacturing process and products must be submitted [RDC 55/2010, Art. 31]. 

For products registered under the exceptional criteria for serious diseases with ongoing phase III studies, the company must also submit the schedule for the completion of phase III clinical studies and any available preliminary results [RDC 55/2010, Art. 23].

